cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this artifact, please cite it and the paper it accompanies once published. This artifact accompanies a prototype-stage manuscript that is not yet submitted (see README.md); cite with that context."
title: "DAIM-OS Self-Healing Artifact: Event-Driven Link-Failure Recovery for a DAIM-OS Client Using OVSDB Notifications and Reactive Path Recomputation"
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Banjar
    given-names: Ameen
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0871-5153"
version: 0.4.0
date-released: 2026-08-12
license: Apache-2.0
doi: 10.5281/zenodo.21910227
# v0.3.0 DOI: 10.5281/zenodo.21909633. v0.1.0 DOI: 10.5281/zenodo.21906993.
# Concept DOI (always resolves to latest version): 10.5281/zenodo.21906992.
repository-code: "https://github.com/ameen-banjar/daim-os-self-healing-artifact"
abstract: >-
  Reproducibility artifact for "Event-Driven Link-Failure Recovery for a
  DAIM-OS Client Using OVSDB Notifications and Reactive Path Recomputation"
  (Paper 3 in the DAIM six-paper programme, prototype-stage, not yet
  submitted). Contains an event-driven repair agent (daim_link_agent.py)
  that subscribes to real OVSDB Interface.link_state notifications,
  recomputes an alternate path with breadth-first search over a declared
  topology graph, installs it through the existing DAIM-OS OVS adapter, and
  suppresses repeated repair processing on a flapping link via an
  IDLE/ACTIVE/HELD-DOWN hold-down state machine, keyed by physical edge
  rather than interface name since v0.3.0. An edge is only treated as
  recovered once every interface observing it independently confirms `up`
  (v0.4.0) -- a persistent per-interface state, not a single last-report-wins
  value per edge. Three real defects are documented in the history: a
  stale-state bug (v0.2.0, found via external manuscript review), a
  cross-interface hold-down gap (v0.3.0, found by the first live-network run
  of the flapping-link protocol against a real Mininet/OVS link -- OVSDB
  reports a link's two interfaces' transitions independently, and the
  pre-fix hold-down only covered the interface that triggered the repair),
  and a last-report-wins edge-recovery gap (v0.4.0, found by code review).
  Contains pure-logic unit tests for the path computation and all four
  hold-down behaviours (suppression, stale-state reconciliation,
  cross-interface coverage, edge-confirmation), all runnable without
  Mininet/OVS; a live-network flapping-link script and its raw data and
  event logs across the pre-fix, v0.3.0, and v0.4.0 re-verification runs,
  retained side by side; the network-level Stage 3 raw data (five autonomous
  link-failure repetitions, plus scripted non-autonomous
  switch/controller-failure conditions) and reports; and the
  figure-generation script and resulting figures used in the manuscript.
keywords:
  - software-defined networking
  - self-healing networks
  - event-driven fault detection
  - autonomous path recovery
  - OVSDB
  - OpenFlow
  - hold-down timers
  - control-plane resilience
  - reproducible systems research
